A||||3||BC-GT||Recent prices fetched by classic muscle cars at auction||||11||
Z||000000||BC-GT||04-30-2001||12:52 PM||||Copied from The Report On Business in the Globe and Mail<P>Stock Performance<BR>Andrew Willis <P>HOT CARS <BR>Recent prices fetched by classic muscle cars at auction<BR>                                                                           <BR>                                                                               Price in 1991    Price in 2001<P>1969 Shelby GT-500........................................... $49,600             $139,200<P><BR>1970 Plymouth Hemi 'Cuda............................... $22,400             $138,000<P><BR>1970 Chevrolet LS-6 Chevelle convertible...... $16,800             $72,000<P><BR>1969 Plymouth Road Runner............................. $10,400              $57,600<P><BR>1967 Pontiac GTO convertible.......................... $21,200              $56,000<P><BR>1970 Ford Torino Cobra...................................... $14,000              $27,600*<P>All prices in Canadian dollars. * price in 1999 <BR>Source: Barrett-Jackson Auction Co.<P>In 1968, Chrysler Corp. engineers dropped a high-performance engine into a sliver of a suburban runabout called the Dodge Dart. For $4,500 to $5,000, a hot-blooded teenager could strap on this land rocket, mash a foot on the gas pedal, and generate enough power to lift the wheels off-road while peeling away from traffic lights.<BR>Today, the boys who got dirty while doctoring the Dart's 440-cubic-inch engine--a power pack twice the size of the one in the minivans they now drive--are revisiting their youth. High-octane adolescent fantasies that were fuelled by television's Starsky & Hutch and Steve McQueen's Mustang Fastback in the movie Bullitt are now achievable--for a heady price. Dodge Darts that survived late-night drag races fetch $68,000 these days, the same price as a spanking new Porsche Boxster.<BR>Baby boomers with wallets that register full tend to head straight for the jacked-up, big-block sports cars of the late 1960s and early 1970s, which is why the muscle-car market has never been hotter. Cars long ago consigned to designers' dustbins, such as the Dart, the Plymouth Barracuda and Road Runner, the Ford Torino, sport versions of the Chevy Chevelle and Nova, and the Pontiac GTO, are all fetching top dollar. There's also big interest in big-engine editions of high-speed street cars that are still in production, such as the Mustang, Camaro and Corvette.<BR>"There's a generation of men who had a passion for cars as teenagers, then spent two decades driving station wagons," said Ty Bennett, marketing specialist for the Indiana-based car auction house Kruse International, which is owned by eBay.com. "Now they've got the time and money to go back and indulge that passion." And how they are indulging. "We're seeing all sorts of people drop $50,000 to $100,000 (U.S.) on a car," says Tim McGrane, director of marketing for Barrett-Jackson Auction Co., an Arizona-based company that specializes in classic and collectible car sales. "Not because it's going to be their second most valuable asset after their home, but because they want a weekend toy that's also going to appreciate in price."<BR>When it comes to collecting muscle cars for profit, rather than just fun, bigger is better. Large engines and big horsepower always command top dollar. Freudians would have a field day with this crowd. Also, all other things being equal, convertibles are worth more than hardtops. And, as with most collectible items, there's a premium paid for scarcity. Most lines of muscle cars saw brief, limited edition unions of enormous stock-car racing engines and juiced-up lines, such as a 1970 variation of the Plymouth Road Runner sold with a 426-cubic-inch engine and known as a Superbird. Find a mint edition of this baby, and you're looking at $88,000 (U.S.) or more.<BR>"We're seeing muscle-car prices rising by about 10% annually, with the best cars probably going up 15%," says McGrane. Three years ago, a top-line 1970 Plymouth 'Cuda, a classic wedge on four wheels, fetched $86,000 at an auction. In January, that same vintage was changing hands at between $105,000 and $138,000.<BR>Those who remain fans of Starsky & Hutch can replicate their heroes' ride for far less. Versions of the '74 candy-apple-red and white Torino that the undercover cops drove now change hands for around $40,000, which means the car has barely kept pace with inflation.<BR>For real fanatics, nothing beats machines with stock- car racing pedigrees. When it comes to winning the hearts and wallets of auto-racing fans, car companies have a saying: "Win on Sunday, sell on Monday." This holds true over many years, even decades, as the top-dollar honours among collectible muscle cars belong to the Shelby GT, a limited-edition car built by renowned designer Carroll Shelby. A 1966 Shelby Cobra 427-cubic-inch Roadster fetched $475,000 at a Barrett-Jackson auction in January. As a gauge of just how fast prices are rising, it's worth noting that three years ago, the same vintage sold for $328,000.<BR>"Right now, you're seeing a limited supply of cars being snapped up by serious collectors and taken off the market, so there's every reason to believe prices for top cars will keep rising," said Bennett.<BR>However, those looking for big bucks on big-block cars should know that in the past, collectors have been in spectacular wrecks. In the late 1980s, when stock markets were also on fire, vintage sports cars got hot. With two-fisted buying by avid collector and former Los Angles Times publisher Otis Chandler leading the way, the price of a car such as the Ferarri 275 GTB4 jumped from $400,000 to $1.2 million (U.S.) in the space of 18 months. Then Chandler and several other major collectors, including baseball star Reggie Jackson, began pruning their fleets. Speculators had to dump cars, and within two years, the price tag on the Ferarri was once again $400,000.<BR>"There are collectors making money doing nothing but buying and selling muscle cars, but they look for just a few thousand dollars on every transaction, know exactly what's hot, and deal in big volumes," says Bennett. The best reason to get behind the wheel of a muscle car is to turn in a blistering quarter-mile drag race, not to turn a quick buck. <BR>||||reg||11
